# The endpoints the account half of this client talks to (#100). Every request below has
# been run against a real control plane, and what came back is recorded under it.
#
# The server answers JSON under /v1, and a session is an opaque token: it is returned once
# by sign in, kept by the client, and sent back as `Authorization: Bearer`. The website
# pages below are the ones the client opens in a browser instead of drawing a form of its
# own, so signing up and resetting a password are not requests this client makes.
#
# What this was run against:
#
#   cd hosted/control-plane
#   docker compose up -d
#   export RFM_DATABASE_URL='postgres://rfm:rfm@localhost:5433/rfm?sslmode=disable'
#   go run . migrate up
#   RFM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8443 RFM_INSECURE_COOKIES=1 go run . serve
#
# The same requests with curl, for a terminal:
#
#   curl -s -X POST localhost:8443/v1/auth/signin -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#     -d '{"email":"ana@example.test","password":"correct-horse-123"}'

@base = 127.0.0.1:8443
@token = paste-what-sign-in-answered-with

### Create an account. The website's /signup page makes this request; the client never does.
POST http://{{base}}/v1/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{"email": "ana@example.test", "password": "correct-horse-123"}

# 200 {"ok": true}
#
# An address that is already taken answers exactly the same, which is what keeps this
# endpoint from listing who has signed up. Signing in is what says whether the account is
# there, and signing up again is not an error the client has to report.

### Sign in. This is the call behind the client's sign-in button.
POST http://{{base}}/v1/auth/signin
Content-Type: application/json

{"email": "ana@example.test", "password": "correct-horse-123"}

# 200 {"ok": true, "token": "ryTxT-R9usDX5gejevUeSwa5VAcsbTffr84o04gFbnY"}
#
# The token is 43 characters of base64url. It is returned here and nowhere else, and the
# server keeps only its hash. The same answer carries a session cookie for the browser,
# which this client ignores.
#
# Set-Cookie: rfm_session=...; Path=/; Max-Age=2592000; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax

### A wrong password, an address with no account, and a password under twelve characters all
### answer the same thing. The client shows it as it is.
POST http://{{base}}/v1/auth/signin
Content-Type: application/json

{"email": "ana@example.test", "password": "wrong-password-123"}

# 401 {"error": "invalid email or password"}

### Who the session belongs to. This is what the client asks on every start, so a session
### revoked somewhere else is noticed at the next request rather than at the next sign in.
GET http://{{base}}/v1/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

# 200 {"email": "ana@example.test", "email_verified": false, "id": "710a5b8c-55e8-427d-8f14-7bb10ad40521"}
#
# email_verified is false until the link in the confirmation message is opened. Nothing
# this client does is gated on it yet.

### The same request with no token, or with one that was signed out, or with one the server
### no longer has.
GET http://{{base}}/v1/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

# 401 {"error": "unauthorized"}
#
# One answer for unknown, expired and revoked. Asking again with the same token gives the
# same 401, which is how the client tells "signed out somewhere else" from "the server is
# not answering": the second one is a connection error, not this.

### Sign out. The session row goes, so the next request with this token is a 401.
POST http://{{base}}/v1/auth/signout
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

# 200 {"ok": true}

### The website pages the client opens in a browser rather than drawing itself. There is no
### sign-up form, no reset form, and no token handling in the client for either.
GET http://{{base}}/signup

# 200 text/html, 2670 bytes: the page posts {"email","password"} to /v1/auth/signup above.
# The password reset page is /reset, which posts {"email"} to /v1/auth/password-reset/request.
